Chapter: LiteralMain
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 THE REPRO FOR FINDINGS.md ITEM 1B: a Real literal whose digits do not fit an
 i64 is read as a different number, silently, with no diagnostic.

 It is an ENTRY rather than a check so that `./harness/metal.py --entry
 LiteralMain` can run it on bare metal too. That is the point of keeping it: the
 zig arm and the x86 arm share a FRONT END, so if both print the same wrong
 number the constant was folded before either emitter saw it, and no plug can be
 at fault. The zig it emits says as much on its face -- the literal arrives as
 `@as(f64, @bitCast(@as(i64, -4349576520114425037)))`, a bit pattern, with the
 digits nowhere in the file.

 Nineteen digits are the boundary: the parser accumulates every digit from the
 first significant one to the last into a wrapping i64 and then divides by a power
 of ten, so a literal is safe exactly while that accumulation fits.

 We say:

Section: Entry

  ck : Text, Real -> Text
  ck (name) (v) = name & " -> " & show (real-to-int v)

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (ck "115292150460684700.0  (18 digits, fits)" 115292150460684700.0)
    print-line-uni (ck "1152921504606846976.0 (2^60, 19 digits)" 1152921504606846976.0)
    print-line-uni (ck "1000000000000000000.0 (1e18, 19 digits)" 1000000000000000000.0)
  end
